    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Patterns and connectivity of the fracture networks largely control permeability of fractured rocks. This paper discusses shear fracture patterns and their connectivity at geometric complexities such as echelon fault steps, fault bends and the so-called Z faults on strike-slip faults by simulating shear fracture propagation paths using displacement discontinuity boundary element method together with a maximum distortional strain energy density\r\ncriterion. It is found that shear fracture patterns and connectivity depend on specific geometric configurations, the applied stress boundary conditions and the coefficient of friction. For a given geometric configuration of the major faults, the patterns of the associated shear fractures and their linkage can be predicted if the state of stress and the coefficient of friction are estimated.The method employed in this study can be used to infer the geometry and\r\ndistribution of the secondary faults from the geometry of major faults detectable seismically.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
